WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.028 --> 00:00:22.035
Marius had lived in poverty for several years, but he had never known true desperation.

2
00:00:22.036 --> 00:00:29.114
He had seen true desperation in the form of this girl—this phantom—who had just passed before his eyes.

3
00:00:29.115 --> 00:00:33.947
She had revealed to him an entirely new and hideous kind of poverty.

4
00:00:33.948 --> 00:00:43.917
She and her family had fallen so low, become so poor, that they could only be described as people living in misery—les misérables.

5
00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:52.163
Every day before her visit, Marius must have seen the girl, or a member of her family, in the hall of the Gorbeau house.

6
00:00:52.164 --> 00:00:57.083
He could hear their voices through the thin wall that separated their apartment from his.

7
00:00:57.084 --> 00:01:03.004
But he had never paid attention. Yes, he had paid their rent, but anyone would have done that.

8
00:01:03.005 --> 00:01:07.164
A better neighbor would have paid attention to them and done more for them.

9
00:01:07.165 --> 00:01:12.188
Suddenly Marius noticed a small hole that he had never seen before.

10
00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:16.656
It was near the ceiling on the wall that divided the two apartments.

11
00:01:16.657 --> 00:01:21.524
"Let's see what this Jondrette family is like," said Marius as he climbed on a table.

12
00:01:21.525 --> 00:01:25.394
"It's not wrong to spy on people if your intention is to help them."

13
00:01:25.395 --> 00:01:31.640
Marius looked through the hole. His room was badly furnished, but at least it was clean.

14
00:01:31.641 --> 00:01:34.172
The room next door was filthy.

15
00:01:34.173 --> 00:01:41.808
The furniture consisted of a cane chair, a rickety table, and two dirty beds on either side of the fireplace.

16
00:01:41.809 --> 00:01:48.197
The dark corners of this room hid who knows what—perhaps spiders as big as one's fist.

17
00:01:48.198 --> 00:01:56.626
A man with a long gray beard sat smoking at a table. He was writing, probably a letter such as the ones Marius had read.

18
00:01:56.627 --> 00:02:04.118
He was wearing a woman's blouse over a pair of muddy trousers. His toes stuck out from his broken-down boots.

19
00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:08.856
A large woman with graying red hair squatted near the fireplace.

20
00:02:08.857 --> 00:02:18.181
She too was wearing a blouse, but hers was worn with a patched skirt. Marius could see a girl lying listlessly on one of the beds.

21
00:02:18.182 --> 00:02:22.094
Probably the younger sister of the girl who had visited him.

22
00:02:22.095 --> 00:02:30.158
Marius was about to step away from his spy hole when the door to the neighbors' apartment opened and the elder daughter appeared.

23
00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:34.920
Wrapped in a ragged old gown, she was wearing men's work boots.

24
00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:39.404
She was breathless and yet there was a triumphant look on her face.

25
00:02:39.405 --> 00:02:46.483
"He's coming!" she announced. "I found him at the Church of Saint Jacques du Haut Pas."

26
00:02:46.484 --> 00:02:49.968
"The generous man?" asked Monsieur Jondrette.

27
00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:59.293
"Yes, he said his daughter had to make some purchases, but he would be along soon. I tell you he's coming now."

28
00:02:59.294 --> 00:03:01.403
"Wife, put out the fire!"

29
00:03:01.404 --> 00:03:08.635
The surprised woman did not stir, so her husband grabbed a broken pot on the mantel and threw water on the fire.

30
00:03:08.636 --> 00:03:14.293
Then, turning to his elder daughter, he said, "Break the chair!"

31
00:03:14.294 --> 00:03:16.888
"What do you mean?" she asked.

32
00:03:16.889 --> 00:03:22.714
He seized the chair and kicked out the cane seat. "Is it cold outside?" he asked her.

33
00:03:22.715 --> 00:03:25.706
"Very cold and it's snowing."

34
00:03:25.707 --> 00:03:32.118
The father turned toward the younger girl on the bed. "Get up, you good-for-nothing, and break a windowpane."

35
00:03:32.119 --> 00:03:37.602
The girl sprang off the bed, trembling. She was speechless at his command.

36
00:03:37.603 --> 00:03:44.118
"Did you hear me?" roared Monsieur Jondrette. "I told you to break a windowpane."

37
00:03:44.119 --> 00:03:51.205
The girl, with a sort of terrified obedience, rose up on tiptoe and smashed the window with her fist.

38
00:03:51.206 --> 00:03:54.094
The glass broke and fell with a crash.

39
00:03:54.095 --> 00:04:00.023
"Good," said the father. He ran his eyes over all the nooks and crannies of the room.

40
00:04:00.024 --> 00:04:04.523
He was like a general making his final preparations before the battle.

41
00:04:04.524 --> 00:04:11.665
Madame Jondrette slowly got up and asked, "Dear, what do you want me to do?"

42
00:04:11.666 --> 00:04:20.173
"Get into bed." His tone of voice showed he tolerated no disobedience. The mother threw herself heavily onto a bed.

43
00:04:20.174 --> 00:04:23.244
Meanwhile the younger daughter was sobbing.

44
00:04:23.245 --> 00:04:26.490
"What's wrong with you?" asked the father.

45
00:04:26.491 --> 00:04:32.419
She showed him her bleeding fist. In breaking the window, she had cut herself.

46
00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:38.149
It was the mother's turn to scold her husband. "See what a stupid thing you've done!

47
00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:41.919
You told her to break the glass, and she's hurt herself."

48
00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:46.562
"So much the better! Now she looks even more pitiful."

49
00:04:46.563 --> 00:04:51.808
Monsieur Jondrette tore a strip from his blouse and wound it around his daughter's hand.

50
00:04:51.809 --> 00:04:58.713
An icy wind blew through the broken window. The father looked around to be sure he hadn't forgotten anything.

51
00:04:58.714 --> 00:05:04.625
"I think we're ready to receive the generous man from the church."

52
00:05:04.626 --> 00:05:12.784
Just then there was a knock at the door. Monsieur Jondrette rushed forward and opened it with many low bows and smiles.

53
00:05:12.785 --> 00:05:21.213
"Ah, my benefactor!" he exclaimed. "Please come in and bring your lovely daughter with you."

54
00:05:21.214 --> 00:05:24.784
A man with white hair and a girl walked into the room.

55
00:05:24.785 --> 00:05:30.705
Marius, who had been watching all this, almost fell from his perch in surprise.

56
00:05:30.706 --> 00:05:37.443
It was Ursula—the radiant young woman who had been the object of his adoration in the Luxembourg Gardens!

57
00:05:37.444 --> 00:05:46.022
She looked just the same, perhaps a little paler. She was wearing a black cloak trimmed with fur and a violet velvet hat.

58
00:05:46.023 --> 00:05:51.506
Below her long dress, Marius caught a glimpse of her tiny foot in a satin shoe.

59
00:05:51.507 --> 00:06:00.570
The elder Jondrette daughter was also taking in the girl's outfit and her charming, happy face, but with a look of evil intent.

60
00:06:00.571 --> 00:06:04.157
The girl laid a large package on the table.

61
00:06:04.158 --> 00:06:11.507
"Monsieur, you'll find here some new clothes, warm stockings, and blankets," said Monsieur Leblanc.

62
00:06:11.508 --> 00:06:17.824
"Our angelic benefactor overwhelms us," Monsieur Jondrette said loudly.

63
00:06:17.825 --> 00:06:26.499
To his older daughter he whispered, "I don't want clothes and blankets! I want money! Which letter did you give him?"

64
00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:30.195
"The one signed Fabantou," she whispered back.

65
00:06:30.196 --> 00:06:33.403
"Right, the actor. Good."

66
00:06:33.404 --> 00:06:38.816
"I see that you are indeed to be pitied," said Monsieur Leblanc, looking around the room.

67
00:06:38.817 --> 00:06:47.721
"Yes, look. My poor darlings have no bread and no fire. The window is broken and in such weather as this!

68
00:06:47.722 --> 00:06:53.205
My wife is sick, and my child is injured. Look at her bleeding wrist.

69
00:06:53.206 --> 00:06:57.448
It was an accident at the factory where she earns only a few coins a week.

70
00:06:57.449 --> 00:07:03.864
I fear the doctor may have to cut off her arm—if we can even afford a doctor."

71
00:07:03.865 --> 00:07:08.784
"Indeed!" said Monsieur Leblanc, clearly alarmed by the situation.

72
00:07:08.785 --> 00:07:15.451
The girl, who believed that she might now lose her arm, began to sob. But her father paid no attention.

73
00:07:15.452 --> 00:07:22.364
He was busy looking at the generous man because there was something familiar about that man.

74
00:07:22.365 --> 00:07:29.348
"The rent is due in a few days," Jondrette went on. "I owe sixty francs."

75
00:07:29.349 --> 00:07:35.224
Monsieur Leblanc drew five francs from his pocket. "That's all I have right now.

76
00:07:35.225 --> 00:07:42.515
I must take my daughter home, but I'll bring you sixty francs tonight. And here—take my coat."

77
00:07:42.516 --> 00:07:46.000
Monsieur Jondrette escorted the Leblancs downstairs.

78
00:07:46.001 --> 00:07:51.340
Marius was desperate to learn where they lived, but he couldn't let the father catch sight of him.

79
00:07:51.341 --> 00:07:55.241
He followed them downstairs and saw them getting into a cab.

80
00:07:55.242 --> 00:07:58.928
Marius looked frantically for a cab but couldn't find one.

81
00:07:58.929 --> 00:08:04.805
As their cab disappeared around the corner, he stood in the street and watched helplessly.
